<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Landlord and Tenant (Ireland) Act 1870</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1870">1870</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Landlord and Tenant (Ireland) Act 1870, Gladstone's first Irish Land Act, sought to protect Irish tenant farmers. It gave legal force to the Ulster custom of tenant right where it existed, entitled evicted tenants to compensation for improvements and offered loans for tenants to buy land, but it did little to stop high rents and evictions and was followed by the stronger Land Act of 1881.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Between the Acts of Union 1800 and 1870 every act of Parliament on Irish land had favoured the landlord against the tenant. The Incumbered Estates (Ireland) Act 1849 had brought in a new class of speculator landlords whose first aim was higher rents, and a report of 1869 by poor law inspectors set out the hardships tenants suffered under them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-ewart-gladstone">William Ewart Gladstone</a>'s Liberal Party had won the election of 1868 promising justice for Ireland. John Bright, President of the Board of Trade, wanted tenants turned into owners, but Gladstone doubted that buying estates for resale would help most tenants.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>From September 1869 Gladstone studied landlord and tenant relations in Ireland and elsewhere in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, and reworked a plan of the Chief Secretary, Chichester Parkinson-Fortescue, to protect the Ulster custom by law. He laid the bill before the House of Commons on 15 February 1870. Its second reading passed by 442 votes to 11, the minority being eight Irish members who thought it too weak and three English Conservatives. An amendment by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benjamin-disraeli">Benjamin Disraeli</a> to limit compensation cut the government's majority to 76. After more than three and a half months of debate the bill received royal assent on 1 August.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Tenants outside the custom, the great majority, gained compensation for disturbance if evicted for any cause other than unpaid rent. Under the John Bright clauses, which Gladstone accepted reluctantly, a tenant whose landlord was willing to sell could borrow two thirds of the price from the government at 5% over 35 years, with no compulsory powers.</p><p>The historian J. C. Beckett judged that the act failed at almost every point yet marked a decisive advance towards solving the agrarian problem. The courts decided where the Ulster custom existed, and the tenant had to prove it applied to him; it covered only a minority of Ireland's 600,000 tenant farmers, and claims for improvements were slowed by a complicated procedure. The historian Philip Bull argues that by making law for Ireland alone the act struck at the traditional idea of the union and at English ideas of property. The whole act was repealed by the Property (Northern Ireland) Order 1997, in force from 10 January 2000.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Landlord and Tenant (Ireland) Act 1870” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
